To prevent the occurrence of acts of Violence Against Women, the Regional Government shall:
a. prepare and socialize matters relating to Violence Against Women;
b. socialize Women's rights;
c. seek to improve education for Women;
d. open skills courses for Women to support the economic needs of the Family;
e. create employment opportunities for Women;
f. provide counseling related to Violence Against Women;
g. empower social community organizations and Women's organizations at the Regional, sub-district, sub-district, and/or village levels to increase Women's knowledge and insight;
h. establish complaint posts for Women's Protection; and
i. establish networks in efforts to prevent Violence Against Women.

Article 7

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 6 letter a include:
a. implementing policies for the Protection of Women from all forms of Discrimination Against Women and acts of Violence;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the Protection of Women;
c. collecting data and information about Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination Against Women as well as supporting regulations;
d. conducting education on matters related to the Protection of Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination Against Women;
e. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the Protection of Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination Against Women;
f. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of Protection of Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination Against Women; and
g. providing support for facilities and infrastructure in the implementation of Women's Protection.
2. To anticipate the occurrence of acts of Violence, the Regional Government:
a. establishes and facilitates the operation of integrated service institutions for Victims by involving community elements;
b. facilitates legal aid assistance and legal services according to the needs of Victims;
c. provides safe houses or alternative residences along with handling procedures, services, psychosocial, and spiritual;
d. conducts continuous handling up to the rehabilitation and Social Reintegration stage;
e. conducts monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for Victims;
f. encourages community awareness of the importance of Protection for Victims;
g. establishes integrated service posts to receive complaints in each sub-district;
h. facilitates comprehensive health services including visum et repertum services and psychological services; and
i. facilitates protection guarantees needed by victims during assistance to Victims.
3. In carrying out obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), the Regional Government takes into account the rights and obligations of Parents, guardians, husbands, or other persons legally responsible for Victims.
4. To carry out obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) through paragraph (3), the Regional Government prepares an action plan for the Protection of Women Victims of Violence.
5. Provisions regarding the action plan for the Protection of Women Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (4) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 11

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 6 letters b through d shall be implemented in the form of community participation.
2. The forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. establishing Family partners at the sub-district and/or village level;
b. establishing Women's Protection units within community organizations;
c. conducting independent socialization of Women's rights;
d. providing first aid to Victims; and
e. reporting to the competent authorities if Violence Against Women occurs in their environment.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and social media.
4. The forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be implemented responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 10

1. The Regent in implementing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women as referred to in Article 9 shall undertake efforts to:
a. coordinate implementation among Regional Apparatus;
b. cooperate with the central government, provincial government, and other regency/city governments in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
c. strengthen the capacity of Gender institutions;
d. facilitate services for the Protection of Women;
e. provide services for the Protection of Women;
f. implement affirmative action for the Protection of Women; and
g. prepare a data system for the Protection of Women including a data system for Violence Against Women.
2. The implementation of policies, programs, and activities as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be coordinated by the Vice Regent.

CHAPTER III

FORMS OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN

Article 5

Forms of Violence Against Women include:
a. Physical violence;
b. Psychological violence;
c. Sexual violence;
d. Domestic Neglect;
e. trafficking of Women;
f. exploitation; and
g. restriction of movement.

Article 14

1. Protection Services to Women shall be provided in the form of:
a. Protection of the Victim's personal security from physical and mental threats;
b. health services according to the victim's needs;
c. medical services for the purposes of legal evidence;
d. confidentiality of the victim's identity;
e. provision of testimony during examination at court hearings without facing the suspect;
f. spiritual guidance services; and/or
g. Protection of Women who work as laborers or workers in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
2. Protection Services as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a shall be carried out by the Family, law enforcement officials, social institutions, and other parties either temporarily or based on a court Protection order.

Article 15

1. In providing Protection Services as referred to in Article 14 paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall:
a. provide services and handling as quickly as possible to companions, witnesses and/or Victims;
b. provide convenience, comfort, safety, and free of charge for companions, witnesses and/or Victims;
c. maintain the confidentiality of witnesses and/or Victims;
d. guarantee justice and legal certainty for witnesses and/or Victims;
e. provide special service rooms at the offices of the Indonesian National Police;
f. provide apparatus, health workers, social workers, and spiritual counselors;
g. create and develop a system and procedures for cooperation in implementing service programs involving parties easily accessible to Victims;
h. provide protection for companions, witnesses, Family, and friends of Victims;
i. provide decent work and livelihood according to the type and degree of disability, education, and ability; and
j. provide protection for female workers or laborers in accordance with the provisions of protection for laborers or workers in accordance with the provisions of legislation.
2. The Regional Government in providing Protection Services as referred to in paragraph (1) may cooperate with the central government, provincial government, other regency/city governments, the community, and/or other social institutions.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be set forth in a cooperation agreement.

Article 22

1. Any person related to the implementation of Women's Protection who knows of the occurrence of acts of Violence against Women but allows it, does not provide Protection to Victims, and/or does not report to the relevant authority may be subject to administrative sanctions in the form of:
a. verbal warning;
b. written warning;
c. temporary suspension from activities;
d. termination of cooperation;
e. revocation of registration certificate;
f. revocation of operational permit;
g. withdrawal of facilities; and
h. administrative fine.
2. Provisions regarding the procedures for implementing administrative sanctions as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 4

Every Woman Victim of Violence has the right:
a. to have her dignity respected as a human being;
b. to Health Rehabilitation;
c. to determine her own decisions;
d. to obtain information;
e. to confidentiality;
f. to compensation;
g. to Social Rehabilitation;
h. to complaint handling;
i. to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
j. to assistance;
k. to health and psychological recovery; and
i. to protection from family, community, Regional Government, and social institutions.

REGIONAL REGULATION OF BANDUNG REGENCY

NUMBER 21 OF 2016

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF BANDUNG,

Considering:

a. that in order to improve the position, role and quality of women is an effort to realize gender equality and justice in family, community, national and state life;
b. that all forms of violence against women constitute a violation of human rights so that women need to be protected in their dignity and honor and guaranteed their right to life in accordance with their nature and destiny without discrimination;
c. that in order to protect the community, realize equity, and justice as an obligation of the regional government to provide protection and welfare for women, a legal foundation is required in the implementation of protection of women;
d. based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on Protection of Women;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions Within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of 1950) as amended by Law Number 4 of 1968 concerning the Establishment of Purwakarta Regency and Subang Regency by amending Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions Within the Province of West Java (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1968 Number 31, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2851);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
